{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'tail -n 130 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790831000000-engineer-linker-L4.claude-opus-5-5.stdout-r2.log'","aggregated_output":"## Fix round 1 (response to gpt-6-sol's withheld L4 sign-off)\n\nAll four items are done and every lane is green. Nothing is staged or committed, and the same 15 files are touched. I appended this section to `collab/1790812000000-engineer-linker-L4.claude-opus-5-5.report.md`.\n\n| Lane (run in the foreground) | Result |\n|---|---|\n| kondo, all 13 changed source and test files | 0 errors, 0 warnings |\n| `bb test:clj` | 2685 tests, 224140 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors |\n| `bb test:cljs` | 2599 tests, 90262 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors |\n| `bb build:yin-repl-peer` | built |\n| `bb test:cljd` (after `rm -rf test/cljd-out`) | `+2554 … All tests passed!`; the new tests are in the regenerated suite |\n\n**1. BLOCKING, the §5.3 gap: fixed with option (a).**\n- **Docs:** the \"Closure by linked requirements\" paragraph is now in §5.3 word for word, and `:yin.link.publish/missing-require-program` (carrying `:module`) is listed in §9.\n- **Code:** `publish.cljc` finds module-level `(require 'm)` forms. For each linked module the collected programs depend on, it also collects the latest program that requires it. Definitions and requirements are collected together until nothing changes, then each program is sequenced once in ascending `t`. If no program requires a linked module, publishing is refused naming that module.\n- **Tests:** a plain test checks that a separately typed require is collected, and that publishing is refused without it. The two-principal REPL test now has P2 type `(require (quote base))` as its own program, and the reader's `(app/g)` evaluates to `43`. Both failed before the fix; the REPL one with `Unable to resolve symbol: base/f`.\n- **Knock-on change:** the collected require program makes `require` a free primitive, so manifests now also declare `require` under primitives. Two expectations changed from `#{+}` to `#{+ require}`.\n\n**2. MODERATE, recovery after a `:materialize` failure: defined, implemented and documented.**\n- **Rule:** rows of committed programs stay in an unwritten set. Every later round that commits, and every name transaction, writes them first, oldest first, and publishes only once none remain. So HEAD never moves over a missing row.\n- **When it catches up:** the failure is cleared, the next publication covers every committed transaction, and the REPL prints a \"caught up\" note. The set survives `(reset)` over a durable store, but not a restart.\n- **Docs:** the rule is in §5.1 and in `yin.repl.dao.space-index.md`.\n- **Test:** the new test first reproduced the reviewer's bug: with the old code, round 3 moved HEAD while rounds 1–2's rows were missing.\n\n**3. MODERATE, Dart key-file permissions.** §6.5 now states:\n- The JVM creates the key file owner-only on POSIX; Node uses mode 0600.\n- Dart cannot set file permissions at all.\n- The operator must protect the file themselves: `chmod 600` it, create it under `umask 077`, or keep it in an owner-only directory.\n\nOn Dart, keygen now prints a warning naming `chmod 600 <path>`. The test checks the warning appears only on Dart, and that the seed still appears in no output.\n\n**4. LOW, HEAD read on every fold.** It is recorded as a performance limit in §11. No cache was added; one would only be acceptable if invalidated on every HEAD change and every index load.\n\n**Mutations, each caught and then restored:**\n\n| # | Mutation | Failures |\n|---|---|---|\n| M7 | requiring programs not collected | 2 |\n| M8 | `missing-require-program` never raised | 1 |\n| M9 | publishing with rows still unwritten (the reviewer's bug) | 4 |\n| M10 | the permission warning shown on non-Dart hosts | 1 |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
